 ### ⚠️ Workflow & Plan Rules
 1. **Plan-then-Approve:** present everything in chat for Ishay's review, in this exact order: (a) **Plain-Hebrew capabilities summary** (🎁 below) → (b) Sequence & Dependency Assessment incl. the Integration Impact statement → (c) Questions & Assumptions triage → (d) **🎤 PM Interview** (section below — its answers are baked into the Decisions Ledger before (f)) → (e) Claude's Optimization Proposals (chat-only — NOT saved into the micro-guide unless explicitly approved into a step) → (f) the full 9-section blueprint. Write `docs/micro_guides/module-[MODULE_NUMBER].md` to disk **only after explicit approval**, containing only the approved 9 sections. **Fresh-context blueprint review (iron rule 2ב extended to blueprints):** before presenting the 9-section blueprint for approval, dispatch an internal fresh-context reviewer subagent (no memory of this turn) that audits it against the iron rules, `STATUS.md`, `db_roadmap.md`, the §6/§7 registries, and "every 🚧 capability carries its `🚧 מN` token with a matching §6 line" — the same adversarial pre-submission check plans get under rule 2ב. **Plus two lenses beyond compliance-checking (ported from a sister project — field experience: these catch what rule/spec-coverage checking alone misses):** the **Contrarian** lens ("what's the strongest case this breaks something that already works?") and the **Outsider** lens (a REG-IN staff user with zero context — does this screen/flow actually make sense to *them*, without knowing how it was built?). Report findings from both explicitly, not folded silently into the compliance findings. **Spec-coverage cross-check (MANDATORY — the enforcement that catches silent omissions; the screen-to-column audit alone only checks numbers already IN the blueprint, so a spec element the blueprint never mentions slips through):** the reviewer FIRST extracts the module's *required-list* directly from its C5 screen(s)+process, C6 table(s), and mockups — every field · action · displayed number/metric · validation rule · process rule — reading the spec blind to the draft; THEN cross-checks that each required item maps to a build step OR a logged deferral (a §7 item / a `🚧 מN`). A spec/mockup item with neither a step nor a deferral is a **silent-omission finding**; an item the blueprint builds but the spec does NOT require is an **invented-beyond-spec finding** (must carry a "deviation from 5.x" note). Mockup-only details are limited-liability — flag them for confirm-intent, do not treat as required. Fix its findings before presenting. Immediately after saving, in the same session: update `STATUS.md`'s row for this module (📘 blueprint ready + link to the guide) and run the end-of-session protocol — a saved blueprint that STATUS doesn't point to is invisible to the next session.
